##  Vector

 A Vector in Java is a dynamic array that is part of the java.util package. It is similar to ArrayList but with one key difference: it is synchronized, meaning it is thread-safe and can be used in a multi-threaded environment without additional synchronization.

Difference between Vector and ArrayList

Synchronization
In a Vector  Synchronized (Thread-safe)
In a ArrayList Not synchronized (Not thread-safe)

Performance
In Vector Slower due to synchronization
In ArrayList Faster as it is not synchronized


Growth Rate
In Vector Doubles its size when resized
In ArrayList Increases by 50% when resized

Usage
In Vector Used when multiple threads access the list
In ArrayList Used in single-threaded applications for better performance




Vector Synchronization

Since Vector methods like add(), remove(), and get() are synchronized, they are safe to use in a multi-threaded environment. However, due to this synchronization overhead, ArrayList is preferred for performance reasons in single-threaded applications.



If you need synchronization with ArrayList, you can use:

List<String> list = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());



Java Code Example: Vector Usage
Here’s a simple example demonstrating the use of a Vector:

import java.util.Vector;

public class VectorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a Vector
        Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();

        // Adding elements
        vector.add("Apple");
        vector.add("Banana");
        vector.add("Cherry");

        // Accessing elements
        System.out.println("First Element: " + vector.get(0));

        // Removing an element
        vector.remove("Banana");

        // Iterating through the Vector
        System.out.println("Vector elements:");
        for (String fruit : vector) {
            System.out.println(fruit);
        }
    }
}
Output:

First Element: Apple
Vector elements:
Apple
Cherry


